 ## Background
-The problem of gaps in chunked volume rendering arises when splitting surface meshes into patches for culling, which increases draw calls and reduces performance if not managed correctly [S1]. The issue is particularly challenging when a LOD system is involved [S1]. Two known approaches to solving this problem are letting cells overlap by one voxel in each positive direction and gathering bordering voxels from neighboring cells to create a seam mesh [S1]. The overlapping cells approach is attributed to reference [6], while the seam mesh approach is attributed to references [8] and [6] [S1]. The source notes that the developed system did not include a complete gap closing solution due to a lack of information on the duplicate edge issue [S1].
+The problem of gaps in chunked volume rendering arises when splitting volume data into separate octants (cells), which results in disconnected isosurface meshes at cell boundaries [S1]. This problem becomes even more complicated when a LOD system is involved [S1]. Two known approaches to solving this problem are letting cells overlap by one voxel in each positive direction and gathering bordering voxels from neighboring cells to create a seam mesh [S1]. The overlapping cells approach is attributed to reference [6], while the seam mesh approach is attributed to references [8] and [6] [S1]. The source notes that the developed system did not include a complete gap closing solution due to a lack of information on the duplicate edge issue [S1].
 
